 relations from the 19th century  yeah it's disgraceful i i mean to be  honest with you now as far as i know  from um colleagues around government uh  they i don't think the government got  much more noticed than the employees of  piano did and my great friend robert  courts the transport minister was in the  commons yesterday and i could see from  the expression on his face just how  angry he was at the way that piano had  handled this um most importantly for the  people who were fired but actually just  it's shoddy to to to do that and to not  give the department for transport and  you notice the fact you're going to do  it because  he would have wanted i think to have  been involved in a discussion to try to  persuade piano to do things differently  is it the case then that there is pretty  much nothing that can be done then from  a government perspective about those 800  individuals it's time for them to you  know i don't know seek employment  tribunals  so sadly it is the case that the  government cannot force an employer to  continue to employ people that the  employer has said it doesn't want to to  employ  but ordinarily in these situations a  company would come to the government in  fact  often companies come to the government  way before they actually need to do  anything because they're just constantly  lobbying for more support in piano's  case it seems that they had made their  decision entirely under their own steam  uh and the dft were told about the same  time as the people working for piano  absolutely horrendous way to treat their  staff  there's and i know there's a lot of  anger amongst ministerial colleagues in  the department for transport they're  seeing what they can do to try to make  the situation better um but the reality  is is that pno have made a commercial  decision um and as much as we disagree  with it um i i fear that for those uh  for those workers um they've been badly  let down by their employer minister i've  got to let you go so you can answer this  as quickly or as slowly as you like um  you know we gave them 10 million pounds  in terms of furlough money can we get  that back from piano ferries  oh no i don't know i mean it sounds like  exactly the sort of thing that um that  if i were the treasury i would be asking  for but um but i i don't know exactly  how these things work i'm afraid i'm  sorry you've got enough on your plate as  a minister always good to have you in  the program thanks for being with me  thank you very much

